# 010 · Published images and a release workflow

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Install from images

## What

Each image carries its version and source commit, as OCI labels and in a variable the services report. On each tag, a workflow builds the control, harness and supervisor images for amd64 and arm64, a CUDA harness image, and the one-container rig image for RunPod on that CUDA harness, and pushes them to GHCR. Claude Code stays out of published images.

Thermite publishes every image itself, the RunPod rig image too: its release checks each published image before it gets the version's tag, and a maintainer can publish a build between releases by hand, for a lab to pin by its digest.

## Why

Rigs on machines, on clusters and on RunPod pull images. Building from source takes about 20 minutes.

## Done when

- [x] A tag publishes every image with its labels.
- [ ] A fresh machine pulls them, and the CUDA harness passes the isolation self-test.
- [x] The images are public.
- [x] The release checks each published image, pulled by digest with no sign-in, before it gets the version's tag: every image reports the release's commit, carries no Claude Code, has no set-id file and sets no key in its environment; the harnesses and the rig image report their image ID and carry neither socat nor bubblewrap; the supervisor and the rig image name no STE checker.
- [x] A build published by hand passes the same checks, and the lab's control pins its rig image by digest in place of the images the lab built itself.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-18, owner: Claude Code is downloaded only when an image is built with that option. It is never in a published image.
- 2026-10-20, owner: The image packages are public.
- 2026-10-21, owner: Control's image keeps the label `MIT`. The image carries kubectl's licence (Apache License 2.0) as a file.
- 2026-10-20, lead: The release also publishes the one-container rig image that rigs on RunPod run, built on the CUDA harness of the same run, so a tester has an image to launch.
- 2026-10-24, owner (relayed by the lab's infrastructure assistant, verbatim): "this seems like a job for thermite agent to build scripting around image management and deployment to GHCR. Its part of its release process." The lab stops building and pushing the RunPod rig image; Thermite's release publishes it, held to the rules the lab held it to (the checks above).
- 2026-10-24, owner: "we will need it for v0.1.0 release we want all proper images published."
- 2026-10-26, owner, on the Linux and GPU checks: "we can have someone else test them later, surely people will try and if it breaks they'll file an issue." They are left to testers after the release.

## Open questions

- Answered 2026-10-21 (see Decisions): control's label stays `MIT`.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-19: A CUDA harness image (CUDA 12.6.3, built from `9041644`) was pushed to GHCR by hand. It stays private until the owner makes the package public. There is no release workflow and no release bundle yet.
- 2026-10-20: The harness image package pulls without signing in. Control's and the supervisor's packages are not on GHCR yet; the release workflow publishes them, and each must then pull without signing in.
- 2026-10-20: The release workflow is built: `.github/workflows/release.yml`. On a `v*` tag it checks the tag against `package.json`, builds the control, CPU harness and supervisor images for amd64 and arm64 (arm64 under QEMU: the pinned Node base is multi-architecture, and the maintainers' compose builds already run on arm64) and the CUDA harness for amd64, from the existing Containerfiles, and pushes them to `ghcr.io/<owner>/thermite-control`, `thermite-harness` (the CUDA image under `-cuda` tags) and `thermite-supervisor`. Tags: the version and the 12-character commit. Labels and index annotations: source, version, revision, title, description, licence. Build arguments: `THERMITE_COMMIT` for every image (goal 012's reported commit), `THERMITE_IMAGE_ID` for the harnesses (`0.1.0-cuda+<commit>`, reported in the hardware report and in build keys; an empty value now counts as not set), never `CLAUDE_CODE_VERSION`. The CUDA base is `nvidia/cuda:12.6.3-devel-ubuntu22.04`, pinned by digest in the workflow. Run by hand (`workflow_dispatch`), it builds everything and the bundle (goal 011) and pushes nothing. The repository has no Helm chart (goal 058 is later), so none is published. Pages: harness image reference (published images), own registry, compatibility, settings, kit manifest, contributing, the contributor guide.
- 2026-10-20: Not yet run: the workflow needs a tag, which the maintainers push. Next: a dry run from the Actions tab, then the first tag; then each new package is made public, pulled on a fresh machine, and the CUDA harness's isolation self-test is run there.
- 2026-10-20: The workflow also publishes the one-container rig image that RunPod pods run (goal 020): `ghcr.io/<owner>/thermite-rig`, tagged `<version>-cuda` and `<commit>-cuda`, for amd64. The CUDA job builds it right after the CUDA harness, on that harness named by its digest: for a tag, the image just pushed to GHCR; for a dry run, which pushes nothing out, a copy pushed only to a registry service inside the job (`registry:3.0.0`, pinned by digest, as in the quick-start test), which ends with the job. It has the same labels and annotations as the other images, under `thermite-rig`, and keeps the CUDA harness's `THERMITE_COMMIT` and `THERMITE_IMAGE_ID`. Its digest goes to the bundle as `rig-cuda` (goal 011). Pages: the image reference (published images, build arguments, the one-container rig image), compatibility, contributing, the contributor guide, the changelog. Checked: the workflows parse as YAML. Not run: the workflow itself, which needs GitHub. After the first tag, the new `thermite-rig` package is made public too.
- 2026-10-20: The release workflow's dry run passed on GitHub for 6bb436b (run 39490863353): control, the CPU harness, the supervisor, the CUDA harness with the one-container rig image on it, and the release bundle. Left: a real tag's run, and the packages public.
- 2026-10-21: The licence label is now an SPDX expression per image: `MIT AND Apache-2.0` for the harness images and the rig image, which carry the Codex CLI (Apache-2.0, with its NOTICE) and OpenCode (MIT); `MIT` for control and the supervisor (`c6295cf`, `tests/release/licenses.test.ts`). Control's image, which carries kubectl, is an open question above.
- 2026-10-21: Built the owner's answer on control's image. Its label stays `MIT`, and it now carries kubectl's licence as a file: `containers/control/kubectl/LICENSE`, the `LICENSE` of kubernetes/kubernetes at `v1.37.1` (the Apache License 2.0; the repository has no `NOTICE`), copied to `/usr/local/share/doc/kubectl/LICENSE` in the final stage. `tests/release/licenses.test.ts` checks that the final stage copies it and that it is the Apache License 2.0. Pages: the image reference and the release notes (`containers/release/README.template.md`); the changelog. Not built here: the control image, which the next compose build or release run does.
- 2026-10-24: The release checks what it publishes. The images job now pushes each image with the commit's tag only. A new `checks` job pulls each one by digest on a runner that never signed in (`scripts/check-images.ts`, through an empty Docker configuration) and holds it to `src/release/checks.ts`: it pulls without signing in, `THERMITE_COMMIT` is the release's commit, no `claude` on the path or anywhere on its disk, no `THERMITE_*` key, token, secret or password and no snapshot key in its environment; for the harnesses and the rig image, `THERMITE_IMAGE_ID` set and no socat or bwrap; for the rig image, no set-id file; for the supervisor and the rig image, no `THERMITE_STE_CHECKER`. The step summary lists each image with its digest and result. A new `tag` job then gives each checked image the version's tag with `docker buildx imagetools create --prefer-index=false`, a copy of the checked manifest, so both tags name one digest; the bundle waits for it. Run by hand with the new `publish` input, the workflow pushes and checks the images with the commit's tag only and makes no release, for a lab to pin. A release's `compose.yaml` now also sets `THERMITE_RUNPOD_TEST_IMAGE` to the release's rig image (`compose.yaml` passes it, empty). Tests: `tests/release/checks.test.ts`; `bundle.test.ts`, `licenses.test.ts` and `machine.test.ts` still pass. Pages: the image reference (published images), settings, the contributing page and the contributor guide (releases). Not run: the workflow itself, which needs GitHub; next, a publish by hand of main, then the first tag.

- 2026-10-24: Every image has no set-id file now, not only the rig image, at the owner's word: the control, harness and supervisor Containerfiles clear set-id bits last in their final stage, as the rig's does, and the build fails if one is left (`tests/release/checks.test.ts` checks all four Containerfiles). The release's check holds every image to it. The command was tried inside local builds of the three images: it clears every bit, and Node still runs. Not built here: the images, which CI's compose and rig image jobs build on the push.
- 2026-10-24: The first publish by hand passed (run 40129571334, `ac86629`, with the owner's go). The first try stopped at the CPU harness's push (`denied: permission_denied: write_package`): the `thermite-harness` package had been first pushed by hand, outside the workflow, so the repository had no write access to it; the owner gave it access in the package's settings, and the failed jobs ran again. Then all five images were pushed with the commit's tag and passed the checks, each pulled by digest with no sign-in: `thermite-control`, `thermite-harness` (CPU and `-cuda`), `thermite-supervisor` and `thermite-rig` (`ac866290ddeb-cuda`, `sha256:9a7672f08d449c3c447b6d1267d8d11216c1eb0a29b4e3731d1bebfd6ea4403e`). All four packages are public and linked to the repository. Left: the lab's control pins the rig image, the first tag, and a fresh machine's pull with the CUDA harness's isolation self-test.
- 2026-10-24: The lab's control now pins the published rig image by digest for both RunPod settings, at the owner's word, in place of the images the lab built; its infrastructure assistant checked the image the same way first, pulled with no sign-in. One difference it noted: the release's CUDA base is Ubuntu 22.04 (pinned in the workflow), where the lab's own builds used Ubuntu 24.04. Left: the first tag, and a fresh machine's pull with the CUDA harness's isolation self-test.- 2026-10-26 05:26 UTC: The v0.1.0 tag published every image with its labels (release workflow, every job passed); each version tag resolves with no sign-in. Left: a fresh machine's pull, with the CUDA harness's isolation self-test.
- 2026-10-26 05:39 UTC: A fresh pull of the published control, harness and supervisor images on macOS, under Podman and Docker Desktop, ran Your first run to the Judge smoke check. Left: a fresh GPU machine's pull of the CUDA harness with its isolation self-test, which testers will report.

